{% doc %}
  blocks/image-card.liquid

  A picture with blocks on it — the panel beside the FAQ in the *FAQ: Banner*
  preset, where a photograph carries a heading, a line of text and a button.

  ## It is a group, and that is the whole implementation

  A card like this is a flex container with a background, a border, a radius, a
  link and a set of child blocks. `snippets/group.liquid` is that, down to the
  alignment swap that makes **Alignment** mean horizontal whether the content
  stacks or sits in a row, and the width pair where Fill means *take what is
  left* rather than `100%`.

  So this file renders through it, with `extra_classes`, `tag` and `attributes`
  — three parameters that exist for exactly this — and adds four things the
  group has no opinion about:

      the shape        an aspect ratio, so the card is a portrait beside a
                       column of questions rather than as tall as its content
      the label        a word pinned to the picture, or set into the content
      the animation    the image family's, through `snippets/motion-attrs.liquid`
      the preload      one hint, for a card that is genuinely above the fold

  Every layout, colour, border and spacing control is the group's own setting id.
  A merchant who has laid out a Group already knows this block, and a change to
  how Fill works reaches both.

  ## Over the picture, or under it

  **Content** is the group's `background_media` setting wearing a clearer label.
  *Over the image* makes the picture the group's background — the content layer
  sits above it and the card takes the ratio. *Below the image* turns the
  background off and renders the picture as the first child instead, so the
  content flows underneath it in normal document order.

  One setting rather than a toggle plus a hidden media type: the group already
  branches on this value, and a second control that has to be kept in step with
  it is a second control that can disagree with it.

  ## The label is not a heading

  It is a word or two over a photograph — "New", a category, a price. Blocks are
  what headings are for, and the card takes any of them. **Show in content**
  moves the label out of its corner and into the flow, for a card whose picture
  is at the top and whose label reads as a kicker over the heading.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  assign content_over = false
  if s.background_media == 'image'
    assign content_over = true
  endif

  comment
    The ratio shapes the card only when the picture is behind the content. With
    the picture as a child it has a ratio of its own, applied by
    `snippets/image.liquid`, and a second one on the wrapper would crop the
    content underneath it as well.
  endcomment
  assign ratio = s.ratio | default: 'adapt'

  capture ratio_value
    render 'aspect-ratio', ratio: ratio, image: s.image
  endcapture

  assign classes = 'image-card'
  if content_over
    assign classes = classes | append: ' image-card--over'
  else
    assign classes = classes | append: ' image-card--below'
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  No ratio when the card is told to fill the height beside it: a box cannot
  both match its tallest sibling and keep its own proportions, and the height
  is the setting the merchant just changed. Decided here rather than with a
  CSS rule that has to out-specify itself.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture card_style -%}
  {%- if content_over and s.height != 'fill' -%}
    --image-card-ratio: {{ ratio_value | strip }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.label_color != blank -%}
    --image-card-label-color: {{ s.label_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture card_attributes -%}
  {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'image', block: s %}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture children -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    No guard on the image here: the snippet renders its own placeholder when
    there is nothing to render, which is the same picture the card shows in the
    other arrangement and one fewer branch than testing for it twice.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if content_over == false -%}
    {% render 'image',
       image: s.image,
       mobile_image: s.image_mobile,
       alt: s.alt,
       ratio: ratio,
       mobile_ratio: s.ratio_mobile,
       sizes: '(min-width: 990px) 40vw, 100vw',
       priority: s.priority,
       preload: s.priority,
       wrapper_class: 'image-card__media' %}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Pinned to the picture unless the merchant asked for it in the flow, in which
    case it is an ordinary child and the gap between blocks applies to it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.label_text != blank -%}
    <span class="image-card__label{% unless s.label_in_content %} image-card__label--pinned{% endunless %}">
      {{- s.label_text | escape -}}
    </span>
  {%- endif -%}

  {% content_for 'blocks' %}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  `styles` rather than a `style` attribute of this block's own: the group snippet
  already writes one, and a tag carrying two of them keeps the first and loses
  the rest — which is the bug `snippets/spacing.liquid` documents at length.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  The placeholder goes to the background layer rather than in with the blocks.
  It was a child, and a child sits inside the padding — so an unfilled card drew
  its picture 24px in on every side and then jumped flush to the edges the
  moment a real image was chosen. The background layer is where the photograph
  will be, so it is where its stand-in belongs.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% render 'group',
   children: children,
   settings: s,
   extra_classes: classes,
   tag: 'motion-effect',
   attributes: card_attributes,
   styles: card_style,
   priority: s.priority,
   placeholder: 'hero-apparel-1',
   shopify_attributes: block.shopify_attributes %}

